<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Allergic contact dermatitis from poison ivy, extensive (L23.7)</content>. Classic linear vesicles after brush clearing, over about 20 percent of the body surface and including both hands. At this extent topical steroids alone are not enough, so a systemic course is indicated, tapered over two to three weeks because a short course or dose pack often lets the rash rebound. There is no sign of secondary infection. [S]<list><item>Prednisone 60 mg each morning with breakfast for 5 days, then 40 mg for 5 days, then 20 mg for 5 days.</item><item>Cool compresses, calamine lotion, and colloidal oatmeal baths for the itch.</item><item>Wash the clothes, gloves, and tools he wore with soap and water, because the plant oil stays on them; the blister fluid does not spread the rash.</item><item>Learn to recognize the plant, and wear long sleeves, trousers, and gloves for outdoor work.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Major depressive disorder, single episode, in early remission (F32.1)</content>. Mood is stable and the sleep loss is explained by itch. Prednisone at this dose can cause insomnia, irritability, mood elevation, or low mood, so he needs closer watching during the course. Psychiatry found no bipolar features. Prednisone with sertraline slightly raises the risk of stomach bleeding. [S]<list><item>Continue sertraline 50 mg daily without change.</item><item>Take prednisone in the morning to protect sleep.</item><item>Avoid ibuprofen, naproxen, and aspirin during the course.</item><item>Avoid nighttime diphenhydramine: sedating antihistamines do little for poison ivy itch and leave him groggy the next morning.</item><item>Collaborative safety plan reviewed. Call the clinic for two nights without sleep, racing thoughts, unusual energy or irritability, or a return of low mood, and call 988 at any time.</item><item>Nurse telephone check in four days on the rash, sleep, and mood.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, depression, bipolar, and mood disorders monograph (2026); Usatine RP, Riojas M. Diagnosis and Management of Contact Dermatitis. Am Fam Physician. 2010;82(3):249-255 (2010)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Nurse telephone check in four days. Return sooner for pus, spreading redness, fever, eye or facial swelling, or the mood changes above. Adult preventive visit as booked, with a PHQ-9. Next scheduled visit 1841-10-27: adult preventive visit. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
